## 4. PostgreSQL SQL Parsing and Understanding

Create a robust PostgreSQL-aware SQL analysis engine.

It must understand complex queries containing:

- SELECT, WHERE, ORDER BY, LIMIT, OFFSET
- INNER JOIN, LEFT JOIN, RIGHT JOIN, FULL JOIN
- GROUP BY, HAVING
- COUNT, SUM, MAX, MIN, AVG
- Aggregate FILTER clauses
- DISTINCT and PostgreSQL DISTINCT ON
- Common Table Expressions (WITH)
- MATERIALIZED and NOT MATERIALIZED CTEs
- Nested queries and subqueries
- CASE WHEN
- COALESCE, NULLIF, GREATEST, LEAST
- EXTRACT(EPOCH FROM ...)
- Timestamp arithmetic and interval expressions
- CAST and PostgreSQL `::` casting syntax
- Window functions and PARTITION BY
- UNION and UNION ALL
- JSON and JSONB functions
- Complex expressions and boolean predicates
- Positional query parameters

Use a proper PostgreSQL-compatible parser rather than relying on regular expressions.

Do not claim full PostgreSQL coverage unless it has been implemented and tested.

On unsupported syntax, show the exact limitation without fabricating a parsed interpretation.

